{
 "lead": "An active filter uses an op-amp with resistors and capacitors to pass some frequencies and block others. The op-amp supplies gain and a low output impedance, so the cut-off frequency and the pass-band gain can be set independently. Two first-order responses, low-pass and high-pass, plus two second-order responses, band-pass and band-stop, cover most needs. The second-order responses are built by combining the first-order sections. They appear in audio tone controls, anti-alias stages before an ADC, and mains-hum removal in instrumentation. A Bode plot gives the cut-off frequency, the pass-band gain and the roll-off.",
 "prereq": "You need: the inverting and non-inverting amplifiers and virtual ground from Unit 6; cut-off frequency and Bode magnitude plots from Unit 3.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "First-order low-pass and high-pass sections",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Build a low-pass from an op-amp in the non-inverting connection. Put R in series with the input and C from the + input to ground. The feedback resistors R_f (from output to − input) and R_1 (from − input to ground) set the pass-band gain A0 = 1 + R_f/R_1. The RC network makes the input to the op-amp V+ = V_in/(1 + j2πfRC). The output magnitude is A0/√(1 + (f/f_c)²). The cut-off is the −3 dB point, where the gain is A0/√2."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f_c = 1/(2πRC)",
     "where": "f_c is the cut-off frequency in hertz; R is the series resistance in ohms; C is the capacitance in farads."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Swap R and C for a high-pass: C in series, R to ground. The cut-off is the same. The gain is A0 for f ≫ f_c and falls below f_c. Above f_c the low-pass falls by 20 dB per decade (about 6 dB per octave) because one RC section is one pole. The high-pass rises by 20 dB per decade below f_c. The op-amp's own gain-bandwidth product limits how high f_c can go; see Unit 3."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Band-pass and band-stop",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Cascade a high-pass and a low-pass to make a band-pass. Each section contributes one pole, so the band-pass is second-order. The high-pass sets the lower cut-off f_L; the low-pass sets the upper cut-off f_H. The pass band lies between them. The centre frequency is the geometric mean f0 = √(f_L f_H), and the bandwidth is B = f_H − f_L. The quality factor Q = f0/B measures sharpness; <a href=\"#butterworth-and-chebyshev-filters-sallen-key-circuits\">Chapter 7.2</a> treats Q properly."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a band-stop, the low-pass and high-pass outputs must cancel at the notch; the band-stop is second-order. Subtract one from the other in a difference amplifier, or use a twin-T notch. A twin-T has two T networks, one RC and one CR, and rejects f0 = 1/(2πRC). The output is near zero at f0 and passes other frequencies."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The band-pass and band-stop curves in the figure are drawn for Q = 1. For a cascade of two first-order sections with f_L = f_H, Q = 1/3, so the peak at f0 is only 0.5 before normalising; separating f_L and f_H raises Q."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Ideal filters have flat pass bands and infinite roll-off. Practical first-order sections have rounded corners at f_c, finite roll-off, and gain errors from resistor and capacitor tolerances. The op-amp adds its own high-frequency roll-off. Keep f_c well below the op-amp's gain-bandwidth product. Use 1% resistors and stable capacitors such as polypropylene for predictable cut-offs."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Reading the magnitude plot",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Normalise frequency as x = f/f_c. Plot |H| against x on a log axis. For A0 = 1, the low-pass curve is 1 at x = 0.1, 0.707 at x = 1 and 0.1 at x = 10. The high-pass is 0.1 at x = 0.1, 0.707 at x = 1 and 1 at x = 10. The band-pass peaks at x = 1; the band-stop falls to zero at x = 1."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "To sketch, draw a flat pass band at A0. At f_c, drop or rise by 3 dB. Then draw a straight asymptote with slope 20 dB per decade per pole. For the low-pass, the asymptote is flat below f_c and falls at 20 dB/decade above. The true curve is 3 dB below the corner. For the high-pass, mirror it."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "|H| = A0/√(1 + (f/f_c)²)",
     "where": "|H| is the output magnitude divided by input magnitude; A0 is the pass-band gain; f is the signal frequency in hertz; f_c is the cut-off in hertz. For the high-pass use |H| = A0 (f/f_c)/√(1 + (f/f_c)²)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "To compute output amplitude at any frequency, substitute f/f_c into the magnitude formula. Convert to dB with 20 log10 |H|. The figure shows all four shapes normalised to A0 = 1."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
